      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Project: Rebirth Facility</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AAjQbR6sB388SiQmyarluXEzJuANy6sMeMcE7xuimLVhmTBQZyrzw61d4E48OGNz8yTS3BMthwhn2fmnpdHFU6fTb1V0xgjpeET5YOolUeN6DWHyOIM_cIkBLBsVpkhIdn6APJbX6pQfAq-9dZd0D4WyoQwEwaG4ifV21sgTaAU_5y6bQEdNLIeM_eepqUynXwloqaxSPXk?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Description: The WWII-era laboratory where Steve Rogers was administered the Super-Soldier Serum.<br>Notes: This placemark is largely a shot in the dark. Half of the accounts of Cap's origin has this taking place in Washington DC. Ed Brubaker's The Marvel's Project is a recent story that builds quite a bit of story around it being in New York, so that's my starting point. When discussing the Manhattan Project with Stark in Iron Man #73, Cap referred to himself as the "Brooklyn Project," narrowing the area further. The storefront facade is usually portrayed as being an antique/curio shop on a side street in a shabby tenement district. According to neighborhood profiles based on the 1940 census, the tenement areas of Brooklyn at that time were the Greenpoint and northern portion of Williamsburg. Beyond that, I tried to pick a period building in an out-of-the-way spot.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1594-757575-labelson</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Description">
            <value>The WWII-era laboratory where Steve Rogers was administered the Super-Soldier Serum.</value>
          </Data>
          <Data name="Notes">
            <value><![CDATA[This placemark is largely a shot in the dark. Half of the accounts of Cap's origin has this taking place in Washington DC. Ed Brubaker's The Marvel's Project is a recent story that builds quite a bit of story around it being in New York, so that's my starting point. When discussing the Manhattan Project with Stark in Iron Man #73, Cap referred to himself as the "Brooklyn Project," narrowing the area further. The storefront facade is usually portrayed as being an antique/curio shop on a side street in a shabby tenement district. According to neighborhood profiles based on the 1940 census, the tenement areas of Brooklyn at that time were the Greenpoint and northern portion of Williamsburg. Beyond that, I tried to pick a period building in an out-of-the-way spot.]]></value>
          </Data>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
